# Terraform working files — none of this belongs in git.
#
# Added 2026-09-15. Before this file existed the repository ignored nothing
# terraform-related at all (the root .gitignore has no tfstate or tfvars rule),
# which made two leaks a single `git add -A` away on the day of the cutover:
#
#   * State. `terraform apply` writes every value it touches into state in
#     PLAINTEXT, including the RDS master password and anything read back out
#     of Secrets Manager. That is why Step 3 has the operator write the secret
#     values by hand and why Terraform deliberately creates `georag/app` empty
#     (deploy/aws/README.md) — committing state would undo that whole design.
#   * tfvars. The six variables with no default are supplied here, and a file
#     an operator fills in under time pressure is exactly where a password
#     gets pasted by mistake.
#
# This is not hypothetical in this repository: a live Redis password was
# committed in scripts/phase0_acceptance.sh and is still in git history.

# State, and its backups. Plaintext secrets.
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*
*.tfstate.backup

# Variable files. Supply the six no-default variables here; never commit them.
# (.tfvars.example stays tracked — see the negation below.)
*.tfvars
*.tfvars.json

# Saved plans. A plan file embeds the same values state does.
*.tfplan
plan.out

# Provider plugins, modules and lock metadata for the local working dir.
.terraform/
.terraform.tfstate.lock.info

# Crash logs can contain variable values.
crash.log
crash.*.log

# Templates are meant to be shared — they carry key names, never values.
!*.tfvars.example
!.terraform.lock.hcl
